extra += ['', '![Reward variants](../results/improved/reward_experiments/reward_success.png)', '', 'Figure 10. Final success by reward variant; variation is across three training seeds.', '', '![Reward training progress](../results/improved/reward_experiments/reward_training.png)', '', 'Figure 11. Rolling training success for the four reward objectives.', '', 'Reduced ladder reward and goal-focused reward produced 9-move routes in most runs, matching board 3 BFS. Potential shaping used F(s,s′)=γΦ(s′)−Φ(s), Φ(s)=s/N for nonterminal states and Φ(goal)=0, with the same gamma as learning. This potential-difference form preserves the optimal policy under the same discounted MDP assumptions. The artificial time limit is a truncation boundary, so strict finite-horizon invariance requires modeling remaining horizon; this is empirical evidence, not an unconditional guarantee. Original reward at gamma .90 also solved this board in these 5,000-episode runs.','','## 12. Ten-seed gamma sweep','','Alpha=.10, epsilon start=1, floor=.02, decay multiplier=.9998, 5,000 episodes and original rewards were fixed across gamma values. The board-3 experiment used ten independent training seeds (301, 307, 311, 313, 317, 331, 337, 347, 349, 353) for Q-Learning and SARSA at gamma .80, .90, .95 and .99. The study was extended to the three required saved board configurations. Every final policy was evaluated in a fresh environment for 100 greedy episodes. Repeated deterministic rollouts are not independent samples; confidence intervals describe the solved fraction across independent training seeds.','','The table gives board 3 results. Wilson 95% score intervals are reported for the proportion of training seeds that solved.','', '| Algorithm | Gamma | Solved seeds | Success mean ± SD | Wilson 95% CI | Moves mean ± SD | Reward mean ± SD | Training seconds |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
