extra += ['', '![DRL tuning results](../results/improved/drl_experiments/drl_tuning_test.png)', '', 'Figure 19. Validation-selected DRL settings compared on held-out layouts.', '', 'Validation could look strong while held-out success remained zero, so checkpoint selection did not establish generalization. The tested DRL settings were insufficient to produce reliable cross-layout performance. Details and raw CSVs are under results/improved/drl_experiments/.','','## 14. BFS, comparisons and limitations','','BFS is an exact shortest-path baseline only because the saved boards are deterministic and their full transition maps are known. It uses the same six legal die outcomes and board layouts as the agents; shortest distances on original boards are 3, 3 and 9. It optimizes move count, not reward. The generalized-board CSV includes a BFS distance for every test board. Random and learned policies use the same environment mechanics.','','![Held-out RL versus BFS](../results/improved/generalization/bfs_test_comparison.png)', '', 'Figure 20. Held-out RL episode lengths compared with BFS minimum moves; failures are counted at the step cap.','','The separate 10,000-episode random audit recorded 9,999 wins on board 3 (99.99%) and one episode-cap failure. A random baseline may be strong on this particular layout because ladders create easy progress; that does not demonstrate policy quality on unseen boards.','','All cross-layout DRL runs used CPU and modest budgets. The gamma sweep varies training seeds on fixed layouts but does not establish transfer. Potential shaping has a finite-truncation caveat. The controllable-dice core task is faithful to the supplied assignment; the optional random-dice variant was not substituted into the core experiments. A follow-up should increase board diversity and interaction budgets, model time remaining explicitly for strict finite-horizon analysis, and evaluate additional unseen boards.','','## 15. Testing and reproducibility','','The final test suite covers environment mechanics and API checks, deterministic board generation, BFS distances, evaluation isolation, normalized observations, potential reward arithmetic, and split disjointness. Final test count and saved-model reloading checks are summarized in results/final_audit/final_completion_report.md. Use README commands to reproduce the separate experimental groups.','','## 16. References','','- Gymnasium environment registry, https://gymnasium.farama.org/v0.26.3/api/registry/ (accessed 2026-11-19).','- Gymnasium third-party environments, https://gymnasium.farama.org/v1.0.0/environments/third_party_environments/ (accessed 2026-11-19).','- Gymnasium custom environment tutorial, https://gymnasium.farama.org/main/tutorials/gymnasium_basics/environment_creation/ (accessed 2026-11-19).','- Stable-Baselines3 custom environments, https://stable-baselines3.readthedocs.io/en/v2.9.2/guide/custom_env.html (accessed 2026-11-19).','- Stable-Baselines3 algorithm guidance, https://stable-baselines3.readthedocs.io/en/master/guide/rl_tips.html (accessed 2026-11-19).','- Rahul Dhawani, snakes-and-ladders (jQuery exercise), https://github.com/rahuldhawani/snakes-and-ladders (accessed 2026-11-19).','- E-JUST course project assignment, Smart Dice Board Game RL Course Project, supplied DOCX in project root.']
